WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:06.410
What forgotten 2000s thriller starring Kevin
Costner was actually designed to be a trilogy?

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:10.599
Which of Liam Neeson's action flicks would
he like to take another crack at?

00:00:10.599 --> 00:00:14.089
How come the girls from Girls Trip never went
on a second trip?

00:00:14.089 --> 00:00:16.600
Keep watching for the frustrating truth.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:22.130
By now, the reputation of the unproduced Hellboy
3 is almost as enormous as the movies that

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:23.540
actually got made.

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:28.600
The story for this potential feature was teased
in 2008's Hellboy II: The Golden Army, as

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:32.980
the titular superhero would've become an apocalyptic
threat to Earth.

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:37.450
Since then, the project has been discussed
actively by the Hellboy creatives, but it's

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:42.920
never materialized, despite especially constant
encouragement from leading man Ron Perlman.

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:49.019
In June 2013, Perlman reaffirmed his commitment
by noting how enthusiastic he was about director

00:00:49.019 --> 00:00:53.359
Guillermo del Toro's vision for how Hellboy's
story would wrap up.

00:00:53.359 --> 00:00:58.809
The expansive scope of a potential Hellboy
3 may have excited Perlman and del Toro, but

00:00:58.809 --> 00:01:01.969
it didn't do much to convince potential financiers.

00:01:01.969 --> 00:01:07.299
In 2017, del Toro officially confirmed that
it was no longer happening, with the focus

00:01:07.299 --> 00:01:12.049
instead shifting onto a whole new Hellboy
reboot that didn't involve any of the creative

00:01:12.049 --> 00:01:14.590
participants of the first two films.

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:19.890
Though Perlman has continued to beat the drum
for Hellboy 3 at least as recently as 2019,

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:23.829
it doesn't appear to be enough to actually
get anything off the ground.

00:01:23.829 --> 00:01:28.289
After years and years of dead ends, Will Ferrell
and director Adam McKay finally delivered

00:01:28.289 --> 00:01:32.659
a second Anchorman movie to the world in December
2013.

00:01:32.659 --> 00:01:36.829
Considering that they managed to turn one
seemingly impossible comedy sequel into a

00:01:36.829 --> 00:01:43.229
reality once, it's not inconceivable that
they could do it again with 2008's Step Brothers.

00:01:43.229 --> 00:01:47.500
And that movie's other leading man, John C.
Reilly, has been very open about the fact

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:50.799
that he's down for more immature antics.

00:01:50.799 --> 00:01:56.539
In November 2018, he told Good Morning America
that there was a concept for a second Step

00:01:56.539 --> 00:01:59.149
Brothers and that he was hoping that it would
materialize.

00:01:59.149 --> 00:02:02.679
"I mean, it requires the cooperation of a
few other people, but I would love to do another

00:02:02.679 --> 00:02:04.970
one of those, yeah."

00:02:04.970 --> 00:02:09.739
This initially seemed like a promising sign,
but in the years since, there's been no more

00:02:09.739 --> 00:02:11.760
word on this potential follow-up.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:16.840
In an even more discouraging sign, Reilly
himself has downplayed the idea of it ever

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:19.159
becoming a reality in more recent interviews.

00:02:19.159 --> 00:02:23.819
Part of the problem is simply the busy schedules
of everyone involved, especially McKay, who's

00:02:23.819 --> 00:02:27.640
moved away from bro-comedies to more Oscar-nominated
dramas.

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:31.720
And John C. Reilly has even more important
characters to play.

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:34.760
"Hi, I'm Dr. Steve Brule."

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:38.970
Edgar Rice Burroughs wrote several novels
chronicling the exploits of John Carter of

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:40.730
Mars back in the day.

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:44.870
So it would make sense that he could also
spawn multiple big-screen adventures.

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:48.360
2012's John Carter aimed to do just that.

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:53.019
Years later, director Andrew Stanton evern
revealed the logos and titles for two sequels

00:02:53.019 --> 00:02:54.670
he'd once planned to helm.

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:59.030
And he wasn't the only one who harbored ambitions
for more, as leading man Taylor Kitsch has

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:02.040
also been eager to head back to the red planet.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:06.189
A few weeks after John Carter launched in
theaters, Kitsch made it clear that he desperately

00:03:06.189 --> 00:03:09.019
wanted to make another movie in this universe.

00:03:09.019 --> 00:03:13.709
His excitement for a sequel was also stoked
by the screenplay for the proposed production.

00:03:13.709 --> 00:03:18.780
Unfortunately, larger forces beyond his control
would ultimately make this an impossibility.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:23.819
John Carter became a legendary box office
bomb, as it lost hundreds of millions of dollars

00:03:23.819 --> 00:03:25.819
for Walt Disney Pictures.

00:03:25.819 --> 00:03:30.620
Any notions of a second film were quickly
scrapped despite Kitsch's enthusiasm.

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:35.450
So unlike the original literary character,
the cinematic incarnation of John Carter will

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:37.800
not go on to have endless adventures.

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:42.700
The world of Speed Racer is bursting with
colors and so many visual details that are

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:46.120
pretty much tailor-made to be explored across
multiple movies.

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:50.650
And the actors in the 2008 big screen adaptation
were quite aware of this.

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:55.450
According to Christina Ricci, who played Speed's
girlfriend Trixie, the entire cast was yearning

00:03:55.450 --> 00:03:59.370
to go for another spin before the movie even
hit theaters.

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:02.170
As she told ComingSoon.net in 2008,

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:06.829
"[The Wachowskis] created a world that they
love and created characters that they loved

00:04:06.829 --> 00:04:08.340
and are attached to.

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:10.790
When we were all leaving, we were like 'write
the sequel!'

00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:11.830
'We want to come back'.

00:04:11.830 --> 00:04:13.090
And they were like, 'I know.

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:14.090
I know.

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:15.090
We're going to.

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:16.090
Don't worry.'

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:18.060
They were as sad for it to be over as we were."

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:22.180
Ricci has maintained that commitment towards
a potential sequel well after the movie's

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:23.180
release.

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:28.729
In 2018, she reaffirmed to CinemaBlend both
her affection for the Wachowskis and her continued

00:04:28.729 --> 00:04:31.800
desire to appear in a Speed Racer follow-up.

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:36.490
But when the film zoomed into theaters back
in May 2008, moviegoers at large didn't share

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:38.210
Ricci's enthusiasm.

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:42.710
The movie was a box office dud, as it lost
enough money to ensure that no Warner Bros.

00:04:42.710 --> 00:04:45.810
executive would ever shout "Go Speed Racer
go!"

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:47.669
again anytime soon.

00:04:47.669 --> 00:04:52.330
Will Smith hasn't packed his entire career
with endless sequels, but he has turned the

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:56.070
likes of Bad Boys and Men in Black into successful
franchises.

00:04:56.070 --> 00:05:01.860
His 2008 starring vehicle Hancock seemed poised
to be his latest hit to land a lucrative follow-up.

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:05.550
After all, movies about superheroes get sequelized
all the time.

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:09.789
True, it may have been an original story not
based on a pre-existing comic book, but it

00:05:09.789 --> 00:05:13.009
did star Will Smith, so why should it be any
different?

00:05:13.009 --> 00:05:18.750
By the end of 2008, Smith expressed excitement
about what future Hancock movies could deliver,

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:22.620
and there seemed to be plenty of potential
in the ongoing adventures of this tormented

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:23.620
hero.

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:26.210
The following year, it looked like things
were heading in the right direction as director

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:32.600
Peter Berg revealed to MTV News concrete details
about the plot, which would be partially a

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:33.600
prequel.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:37.510
Alas, a slew of factors began to prevent Hancock
2 from ever coming to life.

00:05:37.510 --> 00:05:41.569
This included Smith's busy schedule, which
saw him concentrating on other projects like

00:05:41.569 --> 00:05:44.080
After Earth and Men in Black 3.

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:48.810
He was still pitching new ideas for a Hancock
sequel as recently as January 2012, but there

00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:52.070
haven't been any further developments since
then.

00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:55.750
There's no shortage of adventures that the
four members of The A-Team could get into

00:05:55.750 --> 00:05:58.050
in their days as TV superstars.

00:05:58.050 --> 00:06:03.669
So the 2010 big screen adaptation appeared
like it was also made to spawn a franchise.

00:06:03.669 --> 00:06:08.750
One of its stars was Liam Neeson, and considering
that he returned for multiple Taken installments,

00:06:08.750 --> 00:06:12.660
it should come as no surprise that he was
open to the prospect of signing up multiple

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:15.440
follow-ups to The A-Team as well.

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:18.160
As he told Contact Music in December 2010,

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:24.419
"Myself, Bradley [Cooper], Rampage [Jackson]
and Sharlto are very, very keen to do it.

00:06:24.419 --> 00:06:25.419
New York would [be] cool.

00:06:25.419 --> 00:06:27.210
And a couple of other countries too maybe."

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:31.750
The cast may have been ready to go, but The
A-Team property turned out to be less popular

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:34.690
with moviegoers than it was with TV viewers.

00:06:34.690 --> 00:06:39.310
In early 2011, Neeson revealed to Digital
Spy that he was still eager to do another

00:06:39.310 --> 00:06:44.259
movie with the original film's cast and that
20th Century Fox hadn't explicitly said no

00:06:44.259 --> 00:06:45.450
to the prospect of a follow-up.

00:06:45.450 --> 00:06:50.240
However, he also noted that he had a hunch
that the box office results weren't enough

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:52.150
to justify financing a sequel.

00:06:52.150 --> 00:06:54.340
He turned out to be right on the money.

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:59.280
A few years later, director Joe Carnahan officially
confirmed that there would be no sequel to

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:02.740
The A-Team, no matter how jazzed Neeson may
have been.

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:07.860
Judge Dredd dished out some brutal R-rated
justice in the 2012 film Dredd, which managed

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:12.319
to become a cult classic and also helped kickstart
the career of Alex Garland, who would go on

00:07:12.319 --> 00:07:15.449
to direct Ex Machina and Annihilation.

00:07:15.449 --> 00:07:19.810
Despite such notable accomplishments, Dredd
was still a major box office disappointment,

00:07:19.810 --> 00:07:23.009
thus rendering any notion of a sequel quite
unlikely.

00:07:23.009 --> 00:07:27.280
But the Judge Dredd character has overcome
significant odds before in the comics, so

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:31.330
it's hardly surprising that the actor who
played him, Karl Urban, was game to tackle

00:07:31.330 --> 00:07:35.849
the seemingly impossible task of willing a
sequel into existence.

00:07:35.849 --> 00:07:40.550
In May 2013, Urban made his passion apparent
while also encouraging fans to make their

00:07:40.550 --> 00:07:41.550
voices heard.

00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:45.940
So while there was a significant social media
response to make a Dredd sequel a reality,

00:07:45.940 --> 00:07:48.139
another movie never materialized.

00:07:48.139 --> 00:07:52.870
The closest fans ever got was an announcement
of a TV show that Urban was once in talks

00:07:52.870 --> 00:07:55.919
to headline, but that also never came to pass.

00:07:55.919 --> 00:08:00.210
With years of radio silence on the prospect
of a Dredd 2, not to mention Urban now being

00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:05.389
busy starring in Amazon's The Boys, we don't
imagine justice will be served again anytime

00:08:05.389 --> 00:08:06.389
soon.

00:08:06.389 --> 00:08:07.940
"I am the law."

00:08:07.940 --> 00:08:12.300
The Candyman franchise has featured a trio
of sequels, each of them involving Tony Todd

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:16.419
reprising his role as the eponymous urban
legend in some capacity.

00:08:16.419 --> 00:08:21.320
His enduring presence is a testament to how
much passion Todd has for this character.

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:25.300
And at one point, the actor was thoroughly
committed to executing a new kind of Candyman

00:08:25.300 --> 00:08:29.139
sequel that would've chronicled the title
character trying to make a go at life as a

00:08:29.139 --> 00:08:30.600
normal civilian.

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:35.070
In a 2021 interview with Bloody Disgusting,
Todd shared details about this sequel that

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:36.850
he was hoping to get off the ground.

00:08:36.850 --> 00:08:41.130
It would feature Candyman trying to suppress
his violent urges while working as a professor

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:42.900
at a New England women's college.

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:46.500
Living in an environment full of women would've
been a challenge for the character, and the

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:51.260
story would've also included a tragedy that
triggered the revival of his old bee-covered

00:08:51.260 --> 00:08:52.260
form.

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:56.010
Todd was excited for the project, particularly
the striking visuals that were proposed at

00:08:56.010 --> 00:08:57.030
the time.

00:08:57.030 --> 00:09:00.700
Issues having to do with which studio owned
the rights to the property and who would get

00:09:00.700 --> 00:09:04.150
what amount of profits halted the film from
ever moving forward.

00:09:04.150 --> 00:09:08.570
Those issues turned out to be too insurmountable
to overcome, as this particular sequel never

00:09:08.570 --> 00:09:09.570
became a reality.

00:09:09.570 --> 00:09:13.780
"We shall never be separated again."

00:09:13.780 --> 00:09:16.190
Many successful comedies have gotten sequels.

00:09:16.190 --> 00:09:20.670
Everything from Caddyshack to The Hangover
and even smaller moneymakers like Hot Tub

00:09:20.670 --> 00:09:23.110
Time Machine have been granted follow-ups.

00:09:23.110 --> 00:09:28.230
Considering this phenomenon, it's puzzling
how 2017's box office hit Girls Trip has never

00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:29.940
managed to score a sequel.

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:34.640
Making this extra puzzling is how Tiffany
Haddish has constantly talked about her excitement

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:36.970
on the prospect of taking another trip.

00:09:36.970 --> 00:09:41.190
In a 2020 interview with Huffington Post,
the actress revealed that she and her fellow

00:09:41.190 --> 00:09:45.520
leading ladies were dying to reunite for a
sequel, but obstacles kept getting in the

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:46.520
way.

00:09:46.520 --> 00:09:50.280
Initially, the problem was the lack of a screenplay,
before the primary issue then evolved into

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:53.400
disputes over the salaries for the leads.

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:57.570
At the time of the interview, Haddish said
that she was still so excited to do the sequel

00:09:57.570 --> 00:10:01.860
and that she was preparing to pen a draft
of the screenplay herself.

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:06.290
There's clearly a lot of effort invested in
turning this project into a reality, but it

00:10:06.290 --> 00:10:10.510
remains to be seen if that will be enough
to overcome the problems that have been encountered

00:10:10.510 --> 00:10:12.950
thus far.

00:10:12.950 --> 00:10:17.070
Considering that it wasn't a massive box office
hit, there's a good chance you've never seen,

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:22.130
or even heard of, the 2007 Kevin Costner serial
killer thriller Mr. Brooks.

00:10:22.130 --> 00:10:27.010
Nevertheless, Costner was game to continue
to explore the film's macabre world across

00:10:27.010 --> 00:10:32.070
future adventures, as Mr. Brooks was designed
to spawn not just one sequel, but two.

00:10:32.070 --> 00:10:34.180
As the actor revealed to Entertainment Weekly,

00:10:34.180 --> 00:10:37.880
"This was written with the idea that we would
do a couple others.

00:10:37.880 --> 00:10:41.220
When the writers first presented the notion
to me, I said, 'Oh, bulls---!

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:46.130
I haven't done Tin Cup 2, or Bull Durham 2,
or Open Range: The Early Years, so you don't

00:10:46.130 --> 00:10:47.580
have to try to hook me with that.'

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:51.060
But when they told me their idea, the hair
on the back of my neck stood up.

00:10:51.060 --> 00:10:55.100
We're hoping this little movie develops a
following so we can play this story out the

00:10:55.100 --> 00:10:56.320
way it should."

00:10:56.320 --> 00:11:00.000
Costner may have been willing, but the box
office returns were weak.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:04.960
That lack of receipts and the eventual financial
problems faced by distributor MGM ensured

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:08.600
that Mr. Brooks would not be getting a sequel,
let alone a trilogy.

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:11.530
Maybe it's more likely that Tin Cup 2 will
eventually tee off.

00:11:11.530 --> 00:11:12.810
"Well, then do it.

00:11:12.810 --> 00:11:14.730
Quit f—' around."

00:11:14.730 --> 00:11:16.530
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:19.980
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
movies are coming soon.

00:11:19.980 --> 00:11:23.510
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

